.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/acorn-jsx Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "Acorn-JSX"
.PP
\fR\fP \fR\fP

.PP
This is plugin for \fRAcorn\fP - a tiny, fast JavaScript parser, written completely in JavaScript\&.

.PP
It was created as an experimental alternative, faster \fRReact\&.js JSX\fP parser\&. Later, it replaced the \fRofficial parser\fP and these days is used by many prominent development tools\&.
.SH "Transpiler"
.PP
Please note that this tool only parses source code to JSX AST, which is useful for various language tools and services\&. If you want to transpile your code to regular ES5-compliant JavaScript with source map, check out \fRBabel\fP and \fRBuble\fP transpilers which use \fRacorn-jsx\fP under the hood\&.
.SH "Usage"
.PP
Requiring this module provides you with an Acorn plugin that you can use like this:

.PP
.PP
.nf
var acorn = require("acorn");
var jsx = require("acorn\-jsx");
acorn\&.Parser\&.extend(jsx())\&.parse("my(<jsx/>, 'code');");
.fi
.PP

.PP
Note that official spec doesn't support mix of XML namespaces and object-style access in tag names (#27) like in \fR<namespace:Object\&.Property />\fP, so it was deprecated in \fRacorn-jsx@3\&.0\fP\&. If you still want to opt-in to support of such constructions, you can pass the following option:

.PP
.PP
.nf
acorn\&.Parser\&.extend(jsx({ allowNamespacedObjects: true }))
.fi
.PP

.PP
Also, since most apps use pure React transformer, a new option was introduced that allows to prohibit namespaces completely:

.PP
.PP
.nf
acorn\&.Parser\&.extend(jsx({ allowNamespaces: false }))
.fi
.PP

.PP
Note that by default \fRallowNamespaces\fP is enabled for spec compliancy\&.
